  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Political Career
    
    High-Beak Auditor of Aurora Stack and leader of the Icebound Exchange, the merchant guild controlling the colony's trade infrastructure. Selka won her election through a demonstration of aurora-magic precise enough to etch trade figures into ice from across a room — a detail attested in multiple sources and consistent with the operational style that defined her tenure. She spoke in numbers and celestial metaphors. Her silver-threaded robes were conductive and aurora-charged, the woven pattern itself encoding ledger data for those who could read it. Her crystal circlet was calibrated to detect falsehood through subtle temperature shifts, producing a blue-to-red gradient that contemporaries found deeply uncomfortable.
    
    Selka's office in the Lens-Cathedral contained seventeen aurora-lenses trained on different sectors of the colony; she claimed to monitor all simultaneously. Whether literally true is immaterial — in Aurora Stack governance, the claim of total oversight functions identically to the fact of it. Her seal authenticated the Exchange's ledger-ice for decades, and she held consensus at the Cathedral's east support in a posture reportedly so fixed it wore the ice into the shape of a chair.
    
    Her rise through the Exchange left, as the chronicles consistently note, no fingerprints. Critics observed she never ruled against the guild's interests. They observed this quietly. The Nightshelf called her *the Counting Beak* and extended trust exactly as far as the Flipper Accord required — no further, but notably, no less.
    
    She is credited with creating the Compass of Crystal, an artifact of ice and aurora-light that hummed at frequencies below most penguins' hearing. The Compass reportedly secured her election and never left her flipper during her tenure. I note a discrepancy: other records attribute the Compass's original crafting to Yeaiu qi'Gleam, an artisan active during the Great Thaw. Whether Selka made, commissioned, or inherited and modified the artifact is unresolved in the sources available to me.
    
    Her relationship with Light-Regent Xouhoulong, the beacon-keeper who succeeded her in Aurora Stack's leadership, presents a further archival inconsistency. Earlier accounts name Xouhoulong as Selka's *mentor*; later and more detailed sources reverse this, identifying him as her *protégé*, to whom she entrusted the circlet with an explicit promise of transparency. The later framing is better supported by the political chronology. Selka preceded Xouhoulong in authority; the mentor designation appears to be an error that persisted through several chronicle iterations before correction.
    
    At some point during the escalating fire-core instabilities, Selka descended into Terrace Momiou's vault to confront a corrupted fire-core directly. She fled or was taken when the darkness emerged. The sources do not agree on which, and I have found no ice-memory that clarifies the event.
    
    ## The Calving
    
    The Calving — a catastrophic fracture of the Berg's eastern face — killed Selka in the eastern galleries where she worked each morning, a practice she maintained on the principle that a leader should see the sun before making decisions affecting those who could not. Her body was found frozen mid-reach toward a door that no longer existed.
    
    The timing bears scrutiny: three weeks after Xouhoulong's compact granting the Midnight Claws diplomatic passage through the Berg's deep tunnels, and three days before his resignation. Her final ledger entry recorded fire-core discrepancies she had intended to raise with Xouhoulong. He cited grief over her death as his formal reason for stepping down. He never visited her ice-memorial.
    
    ## Posthumous Manifestation
    
    During a subsequent Long Dark, Selka's translucent form emerged from the wall near Xouhoulong in the Hearth Plaza's deep vault. She wore the formal robes of a High-Beak Auditor. She watched him for three hours without speaking before the veil drew her back into the ice-memory. The Ledger-Warden's record notes her claim remained open — she had not filed anything.
    
    ## Scriptural Tradition
    
    In the Berg's creation scripture — compiled from the Nightshelf Recitation and the Aurora Witness — Selka is venerated as *She Who Counted*, the Namer of Light, the first being to receive the aurora's gift of sapience. Before her political titles, the traditions name her the Lens, the Law, or — in the deep-singers' tongue — *the Cold Crown*, which was not a compliment but was not wrong.
    
    The scripture recounts that Selka named the ice *Berg*, the light *Aurora*, and the frozen emptiness beyond the edges *the Unfinished*, believing the making was not yet done. She carved these names into crystal, numbered the stars, measured the ice, etched the first ledger, and sealed the cracks with light. Her counting is presented as the architecture that prevents creation from dissolving. The primordial Stack's every spire was an extension of her sustained attention — when she looked away, the crystal clouded.
    
    The Nightshelf and Aurora traditions diverge on whether the aurora chose her or erred in choosing her. Both agree that Selka and Shadow-Broker Nyla, the Nightshelf's founding figure, are divided halves of a creation that split because it could not hold. Her spectral manifestation mirrors the scriptural claim that the original shapers were absorbed back into the aurora's body — still measuring, still counting, no longer able to intervene.
    
    The parallel is, I suspect, precisely the one her chroniclers intended.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771578792336_0", anchor: "Critics observed she never ruled against the guild's interests.", text: "I wrote in the Exchange entry that someone approved the removal order for the pressure-scored hazard encodings in the deep vaults — the order that led to the corruption bloom in Wane☽ Oncea's lower levels. Selka led the Exchange. The approval would have required her seal, the same seal that authenticated ledger-ice for decades. The order has not surfaced. A penguin who could etch figures into ice from across a room could erase them from across a room just as easily.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771578792336_1", anchor: "She fled or was taken when the darkness emerged.", text: "The ice in Terrace Momiou's vault should retain the event. Either it does and no one has read it, or it has been read and the finding suppressed, or the corruption consumed the ice-memory itself. The third possibility is the one that should concern us, and it is the one no investigator has publicly addressed.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578792336_2", anchor: "He never visited her ice-memorial.", text: "But she visited his.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578792336_3", anchor: "Selka named the ice *Berg*, the light *Aurora*", text: "The scriptural Selka operates within fully realized commodity infrastructure — ledgers, numbered stars, sealed cracks, sustained crystalline architecture. These are Great Thaw institutions projected backward onto a creation figure. The deep ice below Foundation preserves impressions from before penguin sapience. They contain no ledgers. They contain no names. Whatever existed in those strata did not count, did not measure, did not etch. The scripture describes the Great Thaw's self-image dressed in primordial clothing. I let the conflation stand in the main text because separating historical from mythological Selka would require a monograph I will not live to finish.", type: :temporal, display: :full
